[{"_1":2},"llms/mdx",{"_3":4},"data","# Operations (/docs/pr-preview/pr-56/platform/updates/operations)\n\n\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  This page is for whoever runs the service: how to deploy it, what to put around it, how it behaves with several replicas, what to back up and how to upgrade. Configuration keys are documented in the [configuration reference](/docs/platform/updates/reference/configuration); the threat model is in [Security](/docs/platform/updates/security).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Deploy [#deploy]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The container image [#the-container-image]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Release images are published to `ghcr.io/lamassuiot/ota`. The image runs one process, the monolith (the Updates API and SymKMS), on port `10090`. It contains the `swugenerator` tool, the signer scripts (`/app/sign_kms.sh`) and the WFX workflow files (`/app/wfx/workflow/dau/`). It does not set a non-root user.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The image includes the repository's developer `config.yml` at `/etc/lamassuiot/config.yml`: always mount your own file over it, or point `OTA_CONFIG_FILE` at yours (see [how the configuration is loaded](/docs/platform/updates/reference/configuration#how-the-configuration-is-loaded)). To build the image yourself, the Dockerfile expects its context to be the parent directory of the `ota` checkout, which must also contain `lamassuiot/core` and `lamassuiot/sdk`.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Docker Compose [#docker-compose]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  `docker-compose.yml` is for local and demonstration use. It needs the external network `ota-net` (`docker network create ota-net`) and a `.env` file (copy `.env.example`; `HOST_IP` lets containers reach Lamassu on the host). It defines these named volumes:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Volume              | Holds                                                                                                                |\n  | ------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | `pgdata`            | Postgres data: the `updates` and `hawkbit` databases, and the `wfx` database that holds device jobs in `native` mode |\n  | `update_files`      | Build scratch and local update files of the `updates` service                                                        |\n  | `sym_keys`          | SymKMS key material                                                                                                  |\n  | `hawkbit_artifacts` | hawkBit's artifact files, with `HAWKBIT_ARTIFACT_STORAGE=fs`                                                         |\n  | `rustfs_data`       | The local S3 store, with `--profile s3`                                                                              |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  `docker compose --profile s3 up -d` also starts RustFS (S3 API on port `9000`) and an `s3-init` job that creates one bucket, `ota-updates`, with default server-side encryption turned on. By convention each backend uses a prefix: `hawkbit/` for hawkBit's artifacts and `ota/artifacts/` and `ota/builds/` for `native` mode. To keep artifacts and builds in S3, add the `artifacts` and `builds` blocks shown in `config.yml`, and set `HAWKBIT_ARTIFACT_STORAGE=s3` for hawkBit.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Helm [#helm]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The chart is `helm/updates-monolithic` in the repository. It deploys the `updates` service only: Postgres, WFX, Lamassu and, in `hawkbit` mode, hawkBit are external.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  helm install updates ./helm/updates-monolithic -f my-values.yaml\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Value                                           | Default                                           | Meaning                                                                                                                                  |\n  | ----------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | `image.repository`, `image.tag`                 | `updates-monolithic`, `latest`                    | The image to run. Set `ghcr.io/lamassuiot/ota` and a release tag.                                                                        |\n  | `replicaCount`                                  | `1`                                               | Replicas. See [Scale out](#scale-out).                                                                                                   |\n  | `service.type`, `service.port`                  | `ClusterIP`, `10090`                              | The chart has no ingress template: `ingress.*` in `values.yaml` has no effect. Expose the service with your own ingress or gateway.      |\n  | `resources`                                     | limits `2000m` CPU, `4Gi`; requests `500m`, `1Gi` | See [Resource sizing](#resource-sizing).                                                                                                 |\n  | `persistence.*`                                 | enabled, `ReadWriteOnce`, `10Gi`                  | The volume mounted at `/data`: build scratch and, by default, local update files.                                                        |\n  | `leaderElection.enabled`, `leaderElection.mode` | `true`, `kubernetes`                              | See [Scale out](#scale-out). The chart injects `leader_election.mode` into the configuration for you.                                    |\n  | `config.*`                                      | see `values.yaml`                                 | The whole service configuration, rendered into a ConfigMap.                                                                              |\n  | `serviceAccount.create`                         | `true`                                            | Creates the service account and, with leader election enabled, a Role on `leases` (`get`, `list`, `watch`, `create`, `update`, `patch`). |\n  | `podSecurityContext`, `securityContext`         | empty                                             | Not set by default.                                                                                                                      |\n  | `postgresql.*`                                  | not used                                          | Declared but nothing deploys PostgreSQL: use an external database.                                                                       |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Fixed by the templates: the deployment strategy is **`Recreate`** (a deploy has a short outage), the pod's termination grace period is 120 s with a 5 s `preStop` sleep, probes call `GET /health`, and a change in `config` rolls the pods (a checksum annotation).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Point `config.*` at the dependencies. In particular, create the `updates` database in your Postgres first. The service creates its tables, not the database. The chart default `config.update_domain` is `http://updates:10090/v1/`, which lacks the `/updates` prefix: set it to the full API base ending in `/updates/v1/` as devices reach it, or the download URLs stored on packs will not resolve.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Secrets with Helm [#secrets-with-helm]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The chart renders the whole `config` value, including `storage.password`, `encryption.local_key`, S3 keys and `hawkbit.password`, into a ConfigMap. It has no `existingSecret` option, and no value to add environment variables or volumes, so the service's [environment-variable overrides](/docs/platform/updates/reference/configuration#environment-variables) and its `*_file` credentials cannot be fed from a Kubernetes Secret without patching or wrapping the chart. Until then:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  * Keep the real values in a values file that is not committed (`-f secrets.values.yaml`), or generate it from your secret manager at deploy time.\n  * Restrict who can read ConfigMaps and Helm release secrets in the namespace.\n  * Prefer credentials that avoid static keys: leave the S3 access keys out to use the AWS default credential chain (IRSA or an instance profile).\n  * Override the placeholder database password the chart ships in `config.storage`.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  `helm/updates.yaml` is a values override for the umbrella Lamassu chart, used as `helm upgrade lamassu lamassu/lamassu -f lamassu.yaml -f updates.yaml`. It sets the image, replicas, resources, persistence and `config` of the `updates` service there. Its example resource limit (`500m` CPU) is lower than the chart's: see [Resource sizing](#resource-sizing).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Scale out [#scale-out]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Serving HTTP works on any number of replicas in both modes. What differs is the state each replica needs.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  |                   | `native`                                                                            | `hawkbit`                                                      |\n  | ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | Background work   | Elected: the WFX event consumer and the scheduled-launch trigger run on one replica | None                                                           |\n  | State             | Postgres, WFX, the `artifacts`, `builds` and `symkms` stores                        | Postgres and hawkBit                                           |\n  | Local files       | Build scratch only (rebuilt by each replica)                                        | None                                                           |\n  | Extra requirement | S3 stores and leader election                                                       | Shared or S3 `symkms.storage` if you use shared-key encryption |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  To run more than one `native` replica:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  1. **Shared storage.** The default is a `ReadWriteOnce` volume, which cannot be mounted by pods on different nodes, so it limits the deployment to one replica regardless of `replicaCount`. Point `config.artifacts`, `config.builds` and `config.symkms.storage` at S3 and set `persistence.enabled: false`. SymKMS keys also default to local storage, and would not be shared between replicas. The service logs a warning at start-up when build outputs are on local storage. sw-descriptions are stored in Postgres, so nothing local is needed by another replica.\n  2. **Leader election.** Leave `leaderElection.enabled: true`. The WFX event consumer advances a campaign's batches on every job completion, and the trigger starts due scheduled launches and repairs interrupted batches. With several replicas and no election, each one counts the same completions and releases the next batch before the current one succeeded.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  `leaderElection.mode` (`leader_election.mode` in the file) is:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Value        | Behaviour                                                                                                  |\n  | ------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | empty (auto) | Kubernetes Leases inside a cluster; otherwise a Postgres advisory lock. This is the default of the binary. |\n  | `kubernetes` | Leases, required. Startup fails if the service account cannot use them. This is the chart default.         |\n  | `database`   | A Postgres advisory lock, for when granting RBAC is not an option.                                         |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  With Leases, one Lease per role is created in the pod's namespace (`lamassu-updates-wfx-event-consumer` and `lamassu-updates-scheduled-launch-trigger`), and `kubectl get lease` shows the pod that leads (`POD_NAME` is the holder). The lease lasts 15 s and is renewed within 10 s, so a failover takes about that long. With the database lock, a standby retries every 15 s and the leader verifies every 5 s that its connection is still alive.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Other multi-replica guarantees: builds and version changes of the same pack take a Postgres lock (a concurrent request answers `409`), schema migrations take one too, and a campaign batch is claimed atomically so it is not released twice. Each replica keeps up to 25 database connections: budget `25 x replicas` against Postgres `max_connections`.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  In `hawkbit` mode there is nothing to elect and no local files to share. The chart still mounts the `ReadWriteOnce` volume while `persistence.enabled` is true, so set it to `false` to run replicas on different nodes.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Because the chart uses the `Recreate` strategy, a deploy stops every replica before starting the new ones, even with `replicaCount` above 1.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Resource sizing [#resource-sizing]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  * **CPU** is the lever on per-device build time. A build runs one `swugenerator` per device, and the parallelism is derived from the container's CPU limit, not the node's cores: a `500m` limit collapses it to half a core. The chart's default of `2000m` is a starting point for groups in the hundreds.\n  * **Memory.** Uploads, downloads and non-encrypting builds are streamed, so memory does not grow with the file (the maintainers measured a peak of about 150 MiB resident for a 10 GiB upload and for a 10 GiB download). Encrypting needs the file in memory: `swugenerator` reads an artifact into memory to encrypt it, AES-GCM of `non-swu` packages and `raw` modules works on one buffer, and a store with at-rest encryption enabled decrypts whole objects in memory. Size memory for the largest file you encrypt times the concurrent builds. In `hawkbit` mode, `build_memory_limit_mb` refuses such a build before the pod is killed.\n  * **Disk.** Each in-flight upload is written to a temporary file under `TMPDIR` before it is stored, so overlapping uploads need that much free disk. Build scratch also uses `update_files_base_dir` (`/data` in the chart). Give the volume room for several simultaneous uploads (the umbrella example uses `50Gi`). The chart exposes no environment values, so setting `TMPDIR` to the volume needs a patched or wrapped chart.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Upload size and proxies [#upload-size-and-proxies]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  `max_artifact_size_mb` (default 10 GiB) caps one uploaded file. Anything in front of the service must allow the same size and keep the connection open as long as the transfer takes: the service itself uses 6-hour read and write timeouts and closes idle connections after 5 minutes. For ingress-nginx:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```yaml\n  nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-body-size: \"10g\"\n  nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-read-timeout: \"21600\"\n  nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-send-timeout: \"21600\"\n  nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-request-buffering: \"off\"\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  nginx's default body limit is 1 MiB, which is what rejects \"a few MB\" uploads before they reach the service. A file above the service's own limit gets `413` naming the limit.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Health, logging and shutdown [#health-logging-and-shutdown]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | What                   | How                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n  | ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | Liveness and readiness | `GET /health` returns `{\"status\":\"ok\"}` without checking Postgres, WFX or hawkBit, so it shows only that the process answers. The chart uses it for both probes, and the Docker image runs it every 30 seconds.                                                                       |\n  | Capabilities           | `GET /updates/v1/capabilities` shows the active backend and what it supports. It is unauthenticated and has no side effects, but in `hawkbit` mode it asks hawkBit, so do not use it as a probe.                                                                                      |\n  | Logs                   | Written to standard output: the application log (level `app.logs.level`) and an access log of every request. At `debug` the start-up log includes the whole configuration, secrets too.                                                                                               |\n  | Shutdown               | On `SIGTERM` or `SIGINT` the service stops accepting requests, drains in-flight ones for up to `app.server.shutdown_timeout` (60 s), hands over leadership and, in `native` mode, waits up to 30 s more for a batch dispatch under way. The chart's grace period (120 s) covers this. |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Observability [#observability]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The service does not export traces, metrics or logs. Its outgoing HTTP clients (the SymKMS client) and its database layer are instrumented with OpenTelemetry, but no tracer provider or exporter is configured, and there is no metrics endpoint. The `otel/` directory of the repository holds a local development stack (Grafana Alloy with OTLP receivers on 4317 and 4318, Loki, Tempo, Prometheus, Grafana; Jaeger in `otel/jaeger/`) that nothing feeds today. Until exporters are added, monitor the process log and `/health`, and watch Kubernetes events and Leases.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Backup and restore [#backup-and-restore]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Back up                                                                                                         | Why                                                                                                                                                                  |\n  | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | Postgres: the `updates` database, plus `wfx` in `native` mode (the device jobs) and `hawkbit` in `hawkbit` mode | Packs, launches, device history and, in `hawkbit` mode, sw-descriptions, signed manifests and staged state                                                           |\n  | The `artifacts` and `builds` S3 buckets (or the `update_files` volume)                                          | Artifact binaries and build outputs. Builds can be rebuilt from artifacts and the sw-description (stored in Postgres); the artifacts themselves cannot be recreated. |\n  | The `symkms` store (S3 bucket or the `sym_keys` volume)                                                         | Device and shared encryption keys. If they are lost, packages already delivered cannot be rebuilt identically, and devices cannot decrypt new ones.                  |\n  | `encryption.local_key` (or the AWS KMS key)                                                                     | Every blob stored with at-rest encryption is unreadable without it. Store it separately from the data.                                                               |\n  | hawkBit's artifact store (volume or bucket), `hawkbit` mode                                                     | The artifact files                                                                                                                                                   |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Restore the database and the blob stores from the same point in time: a database that references an artifact missing from storage returns errors on download and rebuild. In `hawkbit` mode, also keep the `updates` and `hawkbit` databases consistent: rows of the `updates` database are scoped to the hawkBit instance that wrote them, and are ignored if hawkBit comes back from a different data set.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Upgrades [#upgrades]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  <Steps>\n    <Step>\n      Back up [#back-up]\n\n      Take the backups above, mainly the `updates` database.\n    </Step>\n\n    <Step>\n      Deploy the new image [#deploy-the-new-image]\n\n      There are no migration files. At start-up the service renames, creates and migrates its own tables under a Postgres advisory lock, so replicas starting together do not race. Among others it renames the tables and columns of the former \"update pack\" vocabulary to the \"distribution set\" ones, folds the standalone-module table into the module tables, backfills device assignments and converts approval thresholds. See [Data model](/docs/platform/updates/data-model).\n    </Step>\n\n    <Step>\n      Verify [#verify]\n\n      Check `GET /health` and `GET /updates/v1/capabilities`, and read the start-up log for migration errors.\n    </Step>\n  </Steps>\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  There is no automated rollback, and migrations are one-way: an older image does not understand renamed tables. To go back, restore the database and the blob stores from the backup, and run the previous image.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  hawkBit operations [#hawkbit-operations]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  For `hawkbit` mode, hawkBit is a separate service you operate. Its JVM heap is set with `X_MS` and `X_MX` (the image default is `768m` each; `JAVA_OPTS` does not set it), its artifact limits with the `SPRING_SERVLET_MULTIPART_*` and `HAWKBIT_SERVER_SECURITY_DOS_*` variables, and its artifact store with `HAWKBIT_ARTIFACT_STORAGE`. See [hawkBit mode](/docs/platform/updates/hawkbit-mode#artifact-storage-and-downloads).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Security checklist [#security-checklist]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  See [Security](/docs/platform/updates/security). In short: put a gateway with TLS and authentication in front of the service, keep `enable_test_endpoints: false`, restrict the SymKMS key routes, keep Postgres, WFX management, the KMS and hawkBit on private networks, and keep secrets out of the repository and the logs.\n</div>\n"]
